  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "Evaluation and dataset construction are restricted to two US cities (Chicago and Orlando), which may limit immediate generalization to distinct international street layouts.",
      "what": "A multimodal diffusion framework synthesizes streetscapes conditioned on explicit visual target proportions, text prompts, segmentation maps, and road masks, tested on street-view imagery from Chicago and Orlando. Incorporating visual controls reduced the perceptual difference (LPIPS) by 6% and boosted semantic consistency (mIoU) by 23.7% in Orlando and 46.4% in Chicago, with building view accuracy increasing over 100%. When textual and visual constraints conflict, spatial visual controls consistently override text prompts during generation.",
      "why": "This establishes that fine-grained spatial and quantitative controls can reliably drive streetscape imagery synthesis, allowing planners to visually model precise physical modifications before deployment."
    }
  },
